-- Passage rg7-word-parts-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('bb8241f0-37f6-5ff9-8156-bf2bbffe9f5a', 'rg7-word-parts-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-word-parts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Listening to Volcanoes$q$, $q$Scientists cannot see inside a volcano, but in a way they can listen to it. Long before an eruption, melted rock called magma begins to push upward. As it moves, it cracks the rock around it and causes tiny earthquakes. Most of these quakes are too small for people to feel.

To catch them, scientists use an instrument called a seismograph. The word comes from Greek parts meaning "shaking" and "write." Older versions used a pen that drew a wavy line on a turning drum of paper. Modern ones record the shaking as computer data, but the idea is the same.

When the lines on a seismograph grow taller and closer together, scientists pay attention. This pattern can mean that magma is rising. It does not tell them the exact day of an eruption, but it helps them predict when danger is growing.

Scientists also track how the ground changes shape. As magma collects underground, the surface of a volcano can swell slightly, like a balloon being filled. Instruments placed on the slopes measure even small bulges.

Volcanoes can disrupt daily life far from the mountain itself. Ash clouds can force airports to cancel flights, and ash can cover roads and crops. By sharing their findings early, scientists give nearby towns time to prepare. Good warnings turn a sudden emergency into a planned response.$q$, 'draft')
