-- Passage rg7-word-parts-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('6ba9277d-d8c4-5cce-84b0-02f02a456dc1', 'rg7-word-parts-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-word-parts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Warehouse Robots$q$, $q$In a large warehouse outside the city, hundreds of orders are packed every hour. Not long ago, workers walked many miles each day to find items on tall shelves. Today, small robots do much of that walking.

The robots are automatic. Once a worker sends an order to the computer system, a robot moves on its own to the right shelf. It slides underneath, lifts the whole shelf, and carries it to a packing station. The robots transport the shelves along paths marked by codes on the floor.

At the packing station, a camera checks each item. If a box is crushed or a label is wrong, the system will reject it. The item is pushed onto a separate belt, where a worker decides whether it can be repaired or must be replaced.

Robots are not perfect. Sometimes a sensor gets dirty and a robot will malfunction, stopping in the middle of a path. When that happens, the other robots steer around it, and a technician comes to fix the problem. Engineers study each failure so that the next design works better.

Some of the warehouse's packages stay in the country. Others are loaded into shipping containers to export to buyers overseas. These containers are lifted onto trucks, then trains, then ships.

Many people wonder whether robots will replace workers. At this warehouse, the number of jobs has stayed about the same. The jobs have changed, though. Fewer people walk the aisles, and more people repair, program, and supervise the machines.$q$, 'draft')
