  ('18a9b1bd-b32f-5655-a977-53cf717a3cf8', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-word-parts'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The word "export" combines ex- ("out") and port ("carry"). The word "import" combines im- ("in") and port. How is "export" in the passage different from "import"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"To export is to bring goods in from other countries, and to import is to send goods out.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-prefix-meanings"},{"text":"Both words mean storing goods on shelves inside a warehouse until they are needed.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-prefix"},{"text":"To export is to move goods by ship, and to import is to move goods by truck.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"context-only-guess"},{"text":"To export is to send goods out to other countries, and to import is to bring goods in.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$To export is to send goods out to other countries, and to import is to bring goods in.$q$, $q$The prefixes ex- and im- set the direction, so export carries goods out, like the containers sent to buyers overseas, and import carries them in. The second choice has the right ideas but reverses the prefixes.$q$, 'draft', '6ba9277d-d8c4-5cce-84b0-02f02a456dc1')
